WEBVTT

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:11.970
 I get out to the farm pretty early in the morning, and I like to just sit and just be with the plants and the flowers before I do anything else.

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:22.800
 Because to me, this is not just a job. The Homeless Garden Project is a beautiful, safe space for me to be. I'm not just getting paid to be there.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:30.300
 And so, for me to be at the garden is like my refuge where I can just sit and just connect.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:38.130
 When the work day starts, I get to see so many faces that I've come to love. It feels like a family, working with my coworkers on the farm.

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:46.830
 We've all been through a lot of difficult things that brought us there. And to me, the garden is a place of transformation.

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:58.760
 And there's a mutual understanding between us trainees on the farm that we're all here to really take that difficulty and shape it into something beautiful.

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:09.130
 Welcome to Delicious Revolution, a show about food, culture, and place. I'm Chelsea Wills.

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:15.650
 This season, we're talking with visionary chefs, gardeners, farmers, organizers, artists, and scientists.

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:18.690
 These people have shaped the food movement in California.

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:29.110
 We talk with a diverse group of California's rebel food makers about the ways they do things in their farms, kitchens, and communities that reshape the way we think about food.

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:38.410
 This show is made by Devin Sampson and Chelsea Wills. Special thanks to the support from Cal Humanities, Food First, and Rebecca Murillo for making this season possible.

00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:46.080
 In this special episode of Delicious Revolution, I'm talking to Kathleen, a trainee at the Homeless Garden Project in Santa Cruz, California.

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:51.520
 This episode is a companion to my interview with Derry Ganshorn, the director of the Homeless Garden Project.

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:56.460
 You can find that episode in the podcast feed now or on the Delicious Revolution website.

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:02.650
 Hi, Kathleen. Welcome to the show. I'm so happy to have you here today.

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:09.880
 Today, I'm talking with Kathleen, who is a trainee at the Homeless Garden Project right now in Santa Cruz.

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:22.320
 And she recently wrote this beautiful piece of writing, which she shared at one of the, was it one of the sustained dinners for the project, to an audience of 160 people.

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:26.940
 And we're so lucky and honored to have her come and share that story with us today.

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:28.740
 So thank you for doing that.

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:35.340
 Four days ago, Derry pulled me aside at work and gave me what felt like an impossibly hard task.

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:41.700
 I was to write my story of how I got here and how the program has benefited me and share it with you tonight.

00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:44.910
 I didn't know where to begin and I didn't know what to say.

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:48.020
 My story is one of unfathomable suffering.

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:55.730
 On the flip side, my experience here at the Homeless Garden Project is one of indescribable joy.

00:02:56.300 --> 00:03:03.570
 How could I possibly present these to two extremes in a safe and comfortable way around in just five minutes?

00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:14.280
 Thought about the garden and the wondrous power at home.

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:26.680
 I was subjected to severe, long-term traumatization.

00:03:45.490 --> 00:03:48.570
 There were, however, very few people I felt I could trust.

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:55.840
 My post-traumatic stress made it extremely difficult to form lasting relationships and impossible to work a regular job.

00:03:56.210 --> 00:04:00.400
 I was in a rocky financial situation and terrified of becoming home.

00:04:04.070 --> 00:04:10.110
 It gripped me as I attempted to grapple with my experiences on the immense power of words.

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:18.690
 It can hold space for whatever I have to say.

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:21.920
 And in that space, the words began to flow.

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:31.680
 It was brutal, horrific, and grotesque.

00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:35.120
 At 18, I was miraculously freed.

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:41.540
 Not only was I struck by the beauty of my surroundings, but by the quality of the people who worked here.

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:46.820
 Terry, the executive director, in her quiet, ethereal wisdom.

00:04:47.470 --> 00:04:50.270
 Lindsay, the training and education specialist.

00:04:51.310 --> 00:04:53.330
 And her extraordinary patience and compassion.

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:59.170
 The manager, in his gentleness and humility.

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:05.000
 Angie, the retail manager, in her sharp wit and skill.

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:08.160
 And Mike, the production lead.

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:11.980
 In his quirky, blunt humor and incredible passion for his life.

00:05:12.610 --> 00:05:14.910
 First, as a new trainee, I struggled.

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:20.030
 With the everlasting support and guidance of these amazing people, I began to thrive.

00:05:21.690 --> 00:05:24.830
 A new sense of direction and purpose flowed through my life.

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:29.080
 And internal barriers that once seemed impenetrable gave way.

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:32.840
 For the first time in my life, I felt open, free, and capable.

00:05:33.830 --> 00:05:38.090
 In the loving space of the homeless garden, I have become free to express myself.

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:39.840
 I am grounded in my choices.

00:05:40.790 --> 00:05:46.360
 I have found stable housing and am working to become a tutor, teaching math to underprivileged kids.

00:05:46.990 --> 00:05:51.660
 The friendships I have made here and the intimacy of the bonds I have formed are indescribable.

00:05:51.930 --> 00:05:56.900
 I have a sense of security and peace now, the foundation upon which I can firmly stand.

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:59.900
 I am not, however, the only recipient of these miracles.

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:03.900
 As my dear friend and former trainee, Lori Soderman, puts it,

00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:12.800
 The soil at the garden oozes the time but one of countless individuals whose life has been monumentally transformed by the power of the garden.

00:06:13.150 --> 00:06:17.860
 Whole families have transitioned out of homelessness into stable, secure homes.

00:06:19.450 --> 00:06:22.590
 Alcoholics and addicts have found the courage they needed to get sober and clean.

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:29.510
 People like myself came from the worst of the worst, found safety, empowerment, and self-worth.

00:06:29.510 --> 00:06:38.390
 The thing I love above all else about this project is that whether you are a volunteer, a staff person, a trainee, or simply a friend,

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:40.160
 there is a place for you here.

00:06:40.410 --> 00:06:47.860
 No matter what it is that you need, there is a sentience in this garden that will call it forth and manifest it in your life.

00:06:48.550 --> 00:06:50.920
 All it asks is that you be open, Drew.

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:53.710
 Thank you. That was so beautiful.

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:58.810
 I'd love to hear from you about what it's like to be in the garden.

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:00.100
 What do you do there?

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:02.420
 Maybe you can describe it for us.

00:07:02.420 --> 00:07:06.420
 Well, for the last few months, I've been on the flower crew,

00:07:06.660 --> 00:07:13.270
 which means that I'm in charge of helping to harvest flowers and arrange them into bouquets to sell for community-supported agriculture.

00:07:13.670 --> 00:07:20.850
 And to donate to local organizations, such as the Boys and Girls Club, hospice, things like that.

00:07:20.850 --> 00:07:25.040
 I've done many things on the farm, but I think this is the thing I've found the most passion for.

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:28.050
 I get up to the farm pretty early in the morning.

00:07:29.610 --> 00:07:35.090
 I like to just sit and just be with the plants and the flowers before I do anything else.

00:07:35.470 --> 00:07:37.560
 Because to me, this is not just a job.

00:07:38.200 --> 00:07:44.080
 The Homeless Garden Project is a beautiful, safe space for me to be.

00:07:44.310 --> 00:07:45.910
 I'm not just getting paid to be there.

00:07:46.820 --> 00:07:56.110
 And so for me to be at the garden is like my refuge, like my then time, where I can just sit and just connect.

00:07:56.210 --> 00:08:00.490
 When the workday starts, I get to see so many faces that I've come to love.

00:08:00.490 --> 00:08:03.510
 It feels like a family, working with my coworkers on the farm.

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:09.010
 We've all been through a lot of difficult things that brought us there.

00:08:10.040 --> 00:08:12.280
 And to me, the garden is a place of transformation.

00:08:12.950 --> 00:08:23.680
 And there's a mutual understanding between us trainees on the farm that we're all here to really take that difficulty and shape it into something beautiful.

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:28.290
 And I think the air at the garden emanates that energy.

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:32.200
 And so to me, it's like this vibrant collective space.

00:08:32.440 --> 00:08:33.920
 Well, I have to come visit you.

00:08:34.060 --> 00:08:35.100
 That sounds incredible.

00:08:36.100 --> 00:08:38.080
 Can I spend every day there too?

00:08:38.740 --> 00:08:44.820
 Well, my only other question for you was, what do you feel like is remarkable about this place?

00:08:45.980 --> 00:08:51.670
 When Darian and I talked last week, we talked a little bit about different kinds of service models,

00:08:52.270 --> 00:08:56.140
 what it feels like to do, to do work that matters in more than one way.

00:08:57.450 --> 00:09:05.290
 So when she was describing it, it sounds like she does work that matters for her, which is so important.

00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:08.680
 And you do work that matters for you, which is so important.

00:09:09.030 --> 00:09:13.310
 But then neither one of you are just getting paid for the work that you do.

00:09:13.410 --> 00:09:14.790
 Something else is happening too.

00:09:14.790 --> 00:09:21.680
 So maybe you already answered that question, but is there anything else you have to say about that remarkableness?

00:09:22.020 --> 00:09:28.880
 I feel speechless thinking about it, and I think that's what makes the Homeless Garden Project so remarkable,

00:09:29.320 --> 00:09:36.450
 is that it really is a spiritual experience being there, and it just takes the words out of my mouth.

00:09:36.890 --> 00:09:39.860
 Well, thank you so much for sharing your experience today.

00:09:39.940 --> 00:09:42.180
 It's been really sweet to spend some time with you.

00:09:42.520 --> 00:09:42.860
 Thank you.

